```java
private List<ResponseEntity<String>> race(Callable<ResponseEntity<String>> first,
                                          Callable<ResponseEntity<String>> second) throws Exception {
    ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
    try {
        CountDownLatch ready = new CountDownLatch(2);
        CountDownLatch start = new CountDownLatch(1);
        Future<ResponseEntity<String>> f1 = executor.submit(() -> { ready.countDown(); start.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS); return first.call(); });
        Future<ResponseEntity<String>> f2 = executor.submit(...second...);
        assertThat(ready.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)).isTrue();
        start.countDown();
        return List.of(f1.get(20, TimeUnit.SECONDS), f2.get(20, TimeUnit.SECONDS));
    } finally { executor.shutdownNow(); }
}

private static int status(ResponseEntity<String> response) { return response.getStatusCode().value(); }
```

But ApiException propagates as exception from controller (no MVC). For calls that may throw ApiException, wrap: callable that catches ApiException → synthesize status. Like existing StatusCall. I'll make callables: `Callable<ResponseEntity<String>>` that internally try/catch ApiException and return ResponseEntity.status(e.status()).body(e.getMessage()).

